<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanctuary of Artemis Orthia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> Orthia was one of the main religious sites of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas</a>. Excavated by the British School at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> from 1906, it yielded thousands of votive offerings, including lead figurines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a> and terracotta masks, from the Archaic period. Spartan boys underwent ritual contests there, and in Roman times a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was built so that visitors could watch youths endure flogging at the altar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-artemis-orthia.jpg" alt="Sanctuary of Artemis Orthia" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Bridesmill on en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artemis_Orthia_location_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The cult of Orthia was shared by the four villages that first made up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>: Limnae, where the sanctuary lies, Pitane, Cynosura and Mesoa. Historians believe it probably came after the cult of Athena Polioukhos, protectress of the city. Pottery fragments from the late Greek Dark Ages suggest that worship began in the 10th century BCE, and the first temple is believed to date from about 700 BCE. After a flood in the 6th century BCE the ground was raised on a bed of river sand, and around 570 BCE a second temple and altar of limestone were built a little to the north.</p><p>The second temple was rebuilt in the 2nd century BCE, except for the altar. In the 3rd century CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> around the temple and altar to hold the visitors who came to watch the flogging of youths. Worship continued into the 4th century CE, when non-Christian cults were banned in the late Roman Empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The cult centred on a wooden effigy, a xoanon, which according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> was the image stolen by Orestes and Iphigenia from Tauride. Pausanias tells that after quarrels at the altar an oracle demanded human blood, and that Lycurgus replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> with the scourging of youths, while a priestess held the image. In the diamastigosis cheeses were piled on the altar and guarded by men with whips, and the youths tried to seize them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> saw the rite as a reenactment of an episode of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a>. The cult also included dances by young men, whose prize was a sickle, and by choruses of girls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The British School at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> brought the site to light in its digs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> of 1906–10, led by R. M. Dawkins. Before the dig it seemed to be only a ruined Roman theatre, plundered after the foundation of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in 1834 and close to falling into the river. The excavators found a long sequence of strata, with the richest deposits beneath the altar, including the bones of animals burned as offerings. Carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a> of a winged goddess holding animals were found, made locally, as half-finished pieces show. Over 100,000 lead offerings are now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> collections, and others are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sanctuary of Artemis Orthia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaic Sparta was less austere than legend suggests: the poet Alcman composed choral songs there in the seventh century BCE, Laconian pottery and bronzes were exported widely and the sanctuary of <b>Artemis Orthia</b> received rich offerings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
